# ── Secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# .env holds the real API key, DB password, and JWT signing key. Only the
# committed template `.env.example` belongs in git.
#
# Order is load-bearing: `.env.*` matches `.env.example`, so the negation MUST
# come after it. Reordering these three lines silently stops the template from
# being committable.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
!deploy/**/*.key.example
secrets.yaml
*.dec.yaml

# ── Go ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Build output. Builds happen in a container; nothing here should ever exist on
# the host, but a stray host `go build` must not be committable.
/bin/
/dist/
/build/
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib
*.test
__debug_bin*
vendor/

# Coverage and profiling output
*.out
coverage.*
cover.html
*.prof
*.pprof

# ── Node / Next.js ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# node_modules and .next live on named Docker volumes, never on the host
# (CLAUDE.md §7). If they appear on the host, someone ran npm outside a
# container — that is the failure mode this repo exists to prevent.
web/node_modules/
web/.next/
web/out/
web/.turbo/
web/next-env.d.ts
node_modules/
.next/
.npm/
.pnpm-store/
*.tsbuildinfo

# ── Playwright / e2e ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
e2e/test-results/
e2e/playwright-report/
e2e/blob-report/
e2e/.cache/
playwright/.auth/

# ── Terraform ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# State can contain secrets in plaintext. Lockfiles ARE committed.
**/.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfstate.backup
crash.log
crash.*.log
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json
.terraformrc
terraform.rc
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
!*.tfvars.example

# ── Kubernetes / Helm ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
deploy/helm/charts/
deploy/helm/*.tgz
kubeconfig
kubeconfig.*

# ── Python (Locust load tests, analysis/ notebooks) ──────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
analysis/data/

# ── Load test output ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
load/reports/
*_stats.csv
*_stats_history.csv
*_failures.csv

# ── Local runtime scratch ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.log
tmp/
.tmp/
.cache/

# ── Editors ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.vscode/
!.vscode/extensions.json
.idea/
*.iml
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# ── OS junk ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
ehthumbs.db
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# ── Deliberately NOT ignored (regression guard) ──────────────────────────────
#
# CLAUDE.md — the project charter — IS COMMITTED. An earlier revision of this
# file ignored it on line 1. That was wrong; do not reinstate it. The charter is
# referenced by README.md, by every ADR under docs/adr/, and by the
# `org.opencontainers.image.documentation` OCI label that
# deploy/docker/go.Dockerfile stamps into all six service images — that label
# points at the file's GitHub URL, which would 404 forever if the file could
# never be committed. If a charter revision ever contains something that must
# not be public, redact the charter; do not hide it from git.
#
# .env.example — the committed, non-secret environment template. The `.env.*`
# pattern in the Secrets block above matches it, so the `!.env.example` negation
# must stay AFTER that pattern. Reordering those three lines silently stops the
# template from being committable, which breaks the documented quickstart
# (`cp .env.example .env`).
#
# .dockerignore (repo root) and deploy/docker/go.Dockerfile.dockerignore — both
# are build inputs and both are committed. Nothing here should ever match them.
#
# Verify all four with:  git add --dry-run CLAUDE.md .env.example .dockerignore \
#                            deploy/docker/go.Dockerfile.dockerignore
# and confirm .env is still refused:  git check-ignore -v .env
